1
00:00:03,420 --> 00:00:07,280
Let's check the format for our
respondent data, also sometimes

2
00:00:07,280 --> 00:00:08,900
called our people data.

3
00:00:08,900 --> 00:00:12,360
This data may take the form of a

4
00:00:12,360 --> 00:00:17,080
tab in a Google Sheet, like the
one on the screen, or it may be in

5
00:00:17,080 --> 00:00:17,920
a CSV file.

6
00:00:17,920 --> 00:00:20,780
In either case, the format is the

7
00:00:20,780 --> 00:00:21,040
same.

8
00:00:21,040 --> 00:00:25,720
First off, there should be a

9
00:00:25,720 --> 00:00:29,520
column corresponding to a unique
ID for all of the people who have

10
00:00:29,520 --> 00:00:29,860
responded.

11
00:00:29,860 --> 00:00:33,040
So you'll see this is in column A

12
00:00:33,040 --> 00:00:37,740
We have unique IDs for the 100
people who have responded and

13
00:00:37,740 --> 00:00:42,460
shown interest in our democratic
lottery Next there should be a

14
00:00:42,460 --> 00:00:48,120
series of columns that correspond
to our targets So these are the

15
00:00:48,120 --> 00:00:51,020
columns shown in red We've got
gender, age bucket, ethnicity and

16
00:00:51,020 --> 00:00:56,840
disability You need to check that
these correspond to your target

17
00:00:56,840 --> 00:01:00,120
data listed under the category
column of your target data.

18
00:01:01,550 --> 00:01:04,910
So here again, we have gender, age
bucket, ethnicity, disability,

19
00:01:05,209 --> 00:01:07,750
exactly the same as in our
respondent data.

20
00:01:07,750 --> 00:01:12,210
Now, there may be some other
columns.

21
00:01:12,210 --> 00:01:17,090
For instance, you may need to know
the person's name and their

22
00:01:17,090 --> 00:01:18,490
details for contacting them.

23
00:01:18,490 --> 00:01:20,030
But those columns do not directly

24
00:01:20,030 --> 00:01:22,430
affect how we run our selection.

25
00:01:22,430 --> 00:01:25,690
The key columns for us are the

26
00:01:25,690 --> 00:01:29,330
ones that we're going to use for
targets.

27
00:01:29,330 --> 00:01:31,360
So now take, for example, the
gender column.

28
00:01:31,360 --> 00:01:34,300
The entries in the gender column
must correspond exactly to the

29
00:01:34,400 --> 00:01:37,640
name values listed in your target
data.

30
00:01:37,640 --> 00:01:43,380
So here, under gender, our
possible values are female, male,

31
00:01:43,380 --> 00:01:45,820
non -binary, or other.

32
00:01:45,820 --> 00:01:48,600
And you'll see in the respondents

33
00:01:48,820 --> 00:01:54,680
data, under the gender heading,
All of the entries are either

34
00:01:54,760 --> 00:01:59,280
female, male or non -binary or
other Let's check another one If

35
00:01:59,280 --> 00:02:05,940
we look at age bucket and we move
across to our target data You'll

36
00:02:05,940 --> 00:02:11,080
see that there are various ranges
that are allowed 0 to 17, 18 to 24

37
00:02:11,080 --> 00:02:16,440
and so on If we check back in our
respondents data You'll see that

38
00:02:16,440 --> 00:02:20,380
all of these entries correspond to
those listed in that category.

39
00:02:20,380 --> 00:02:24,100
And the same for all of our target
columns.

